The deliberation remains centered on whether the primary limitation is the inability of bulk experiments to capture conformational heterogeneity (Choice C) or the standard thermodynamic assumption that the heat capacity change upon denaturation is zero (Choice A). Proponents of Choice A emphasize that NNBP parameterization relies on short, two-state duplexes specifically designed to minimize heterogeneity, making the zero heat capacity change assumption the main source of extrapolation error from melting temperatures to physiological conditions even under ideal experimental settings. Proponents of Choice C argue that ensemble averaging is the fundamental, inescapable physical limit distinguishing bulk measurements from single-molecule techniques. Current evidence from the literature on nucleic acid thermodynamics and single-molecule unzipping strongly favors Choice A as the specific limitation impacting nearest-neighbor parameter derivation.
}

